1:["$","$Ld",null,{"page":{"id":"library--character-study","kind":"library-article","route":"/roadmap/library/character-study","sourceUrl":"https://refold.la/roadmap/library/character-study","sourceHash":"2f0116aa8db681b76d04f451a90b33c9b0e17d583f80e8ee5504bbd8ff82932d","collectedAt":"2026-09-01T16:52:37.389Z","bodyMode":"authorized-full-body","title":{"en":"Character Study","zh":"汉字学习"},"description":{"en":"","zh":""},"segments":[{"id":"segment-0001","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"This article is specifically about learning <strong>Chinese characters</strong> — known as hanzi in Chinese and kanji in Japanese. If your target language uses a phonetic writing system (like Cyrillic, Arabic, Korean hangul, or Japanese kana), see the article on writing system study instead. <a href=\"/roadmap/library/writing-system-study\">Writing System Study</a>","zh":"本文专门介绍如何学习<strong>汉字</strong>——中文称为 hanzi，日文称为 kanji。如果你的目标语言使用表音文字系统（如西里尔字母、阿拉伯字母、韩文谚文或日文假名），请改为参阅文字系统学习一文。<a href=\"/roadmap/library/writing-system-study\">文字系统学习</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0002","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Characters are fundamentally different from alphabets. An alphabet represents the <em>sounds</em> of a word — the letters S-P-E-L-L-I-N-G represent how the word is pronounced, which reminds you of the meaning. A character works the other way around: it represents the <em>meaning</em> (or an idea), and you have to already know the word to know how it's pronounced.","zh":"汉字与字母有着根本性的区别。字母表示单词的<em>读音</em>——S-P-E-L-L-I-N-G 这些字母表示这个词如何发音，进而让你想起它的含义。汉字的运作方式正好相反：它表示<em>含义</em>（或某个概念），而你必须事先知道这个词，才能知道它如何发音。"}},{"id":"segment-0003","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"A useful comparison is emojis. Pretty much everyone would see 🐕 and think \"dog\" without needing it spelled out. Characters work similarly — they represent ideas, not sounds. But unlike emojis, characters are a full writing system used for everything.","zh":"一个很有用的类比是表情符号。几乎所有人看到 🐕 都会想到“狗”，不需要把这个词拼写出来。汉字的运作方式与此类似——它们表示概念，而非读音。但与表情符号不同，汉字是一套用于表达一切内容的完整文字系统。"}},{"id":"key-concepts","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Key Concepts","zh":"关键概念"}},{"id":"segment-0005","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>A character does not equal a word.</strong> A single character might be a word on its own, but it also might not be. One character can appear in many different words with related (or sometimes unrelated) meanings. And many words are made up of two or more characters combined. For example, the character for \"life/birth\" (生) and the character for \"study\" (学) combine to form \"student\" (学生).","zh":"<strong>一个汉字并不等于一个词。</strong>单个汉字本身可能是一个词，也可能不是。同一个汉字可以出现在许多不同的词中，这些词的含义可能相关，有时也可能毫无关联。很多词则由两个或更多汉字组合而成。例如，表示“生命／出生”的“生”和表示“学习”的“学”组合起来，就构成了“学生”一词。"}},{"id":"segment-0006","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Characters (mostly) don't contain pronunciation.</strong> Unlike an alphabet where you can sound out an unfamiliar word, seeing an unfamiliar character gives you almost no information about how to say it. This is why helper text (pinyin for Mandarin, furigana for Japanese) is so critical during learning.","zh":"<strong>汉字（大多）不包含读音信息。</strong>使用字母时，你可以尝试拼读一个陌生词；但看到一个陌生汉字时，你几乎无法得知它该怎么读。因此，学习过程中辅助注音文字（普通话的拼音、日语的振假名）至关重要。"}},{"id":"segment-0007","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Characters almost always equal one syllable.</strong> In Chinese, this is consistent — one character, one syllable. In Japanese, kanji can represent multiple syllables and are often followed by kana (hiragana characters) that modify the word's meaning — for example, conjugating a verb.","zh":"<strong>汉字几乎总是对应一个音节。</strong>中文在这一点上很一致——一个汉字对应一个音节。日语汉字则可以表示多个音节，后面还经常跟着用于改变词义或语法功能的假名（平假名），例如用于动词变形。"}},{"id":"segment-0008","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>No spaces.</strong> Chinese and Japanese don't use spaces between words. You have to know the language well enough to mentally segment the text. This sounds terrifying, but it's actually something that develops naturally as you spend time reading.","zh":"<strong>没有空格。</strong>中文和日文不会在词与词之间使用空格。你必须足够熟悉这门语言，才能在脑中对文本进行分词。这听起来很吓人，但随着阅读时间的增加，这项能力其实会自然形成。"}},{"id":"components-and-radicals","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Components and Radicals","zh":"部件与部首"}},{"id":"segment-0010","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Characters are built from smaller pieces. A <strong>stroke</strong> is the most fundamental building block — a single line or curve. Multiple strokes combine into <strong>radicals</strong> and <strong>components</strong>. Components are the useful building blocks to pay attention to, because they often carry a hint about the character's meaning.","zh":"汉字由更小的部分构成。<strong>笔画</strong>是最基本的构成单位，即一条线或一道弯。多道笔画会组合成<strong>部首</strong>和<strong>部件</strong>。部件是值得留意的实用构成单位，因为它们往往能提示汉字的含义。"}},{"id":"segment-0011","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"For example, the \"mouth\" component (口) appears in characters related to eating, shouting, and other mouth-related activities. These hints aren't perfect — you can't reliably guess a character's meaning from its components alone — but they help you remember and distinguish characters over time.","zh":"例如，“口”部件会出现在与进食、喊叫以及其他口部活动相关的汉字中。这些提示并不完美——你无法仅凭部件可靠地猜出一个汉字的含义——但随着时间推移，它们能帮助你记住和区分汉字。"}},{"id":"segment-0012","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"You don't need to formally study components or radicals. You'll naturally start recognizing them as you spend time reading. Some learners find it helpful to learn the most common components early on, but it's not required.","zh":"你不必系统学习部件或部首。随着阅读时间的增加，你自然会开始认出它们。有些学习者认为尽早学习最常见的部件很有帮助，但这并非必需。"}},{"id":"how-to-learn-characters","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"How to Learn Characters","zh":"如何学习汉字"}},{"id":"segment-0014","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The core approach is simple: <strong>learn the words first, and the characters come along for the ride.</strong>","zh":"核心方法很简单：<strong>先学词，汉字自然会随之掌握。</strong>"}},{"id":"segment-0015","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"This means you should focus on learning vocabulary — the meaning and pronunciation of words — using audio and helper text (pinyin/furigana). The character is there in the background, and as you see it again and again across different words and contexts, you'll naturally learn to recognize it. <a href=\"/roadmap/library/learning-words-with-anki\">Learning words with Anki</a>","zh":"这意味着你应该借助音频和辅助注音文字（拼音／振假名），专注于学习词汇，也就是词语的含义和读音。汉字会同时出现在背景中；当你在不同词语和语境中一遍又一遍地看到它时，自然就会学会辨认。<a href=\"/roadmap/library/learning-words-with-anki\">使用 Anki 学习词语</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0016","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"You don't need to do dedicated \"character study\" where you memorize characters in isolation. Thousands of learners before you have tried that approach and found it inefficient. Anki plus reading and listening will get you very far without any extra effort specifically targeting characters.","zh":"你不需要进行专门的“汉字学习”，孤立地背诵汉字。此前已有数以千计的学习者尝试过这种方法，并发现效率不高。通过 Anki，再配合阅读和听力练习，即使不额外花力气专攻汉字，你也能取得长足进步。"}},{"id":"the-order-of-operations","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"The Order of Operations","zh":"学习步骤"}},{"id":"segment-0018","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Here's a practical progression for handling characters during your learning:","zh":"下面是一套在学习过程中逐步掌握汉字的实用流程："}},{"id":"segment-0019","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Phase 1 (first ~150–200 words):</strong> Learn words using audio, the phonetic writing system (pinyin/furigana), and characters all together. The character is just an image at this point — you're not trying to read it. Focus on learning the word's meaning and sound.","zh":"<strong>阶段 1（最初约 150～200 个词）：</strong>结合音频、表音文字系统（拼音／振假名）和汉字一起学习词语。此时，汉字对你来说只是一幅图像——你并不是在尝试阅读它。请专注于学习词语的含义和读音。"}},{"id":"segment-0020","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Phase 2 (until ~500 words known):</strong> Remove the phonetic writing from your Anki cards, but keep audio. You now see the character and hear the word, and try to remember the meaning. If you forget, the phonetic text is on the back of the card. During immersion, keep both the characters and phonetic text visible.","zh":"<strong>阶段 2（直到认识约 500 个词）：</strong>从 Anki 卡片正面移除表音文字，但保留音频。现在你会看到汉字、听到词语，然后尝试回忆其含义。如果忘记了，卡片背面仍有注音文字。在沉浸过程中，继续同时显示汉字和注音文字。"}},{"id":"segment-0021","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Phase 3 (until ~2000+ words known):</strong> Remove the phonetic writing from your immersion materials too. You now read characters with audio support. On your Anki cards, try to recall the pronunciation before hearing it, but don't grade yourself on pronunciation yet — just on meaning.","zh":"<strong>阶段 3（直到认识约 2000 个以上的词）：</strong>也从沉浸材料中移除表音文字。现在你会在音频辅助下阅读汉字。在 Anki 卡片上，听到音频前先尝试回忆读音，但暂时不要根据读音给自己评分——只根据含义评分。"}},{"id":"segment-0022","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Phase 4 (after ~2000+ words known):</strong> At this point, you start grading yourself on whether you can recall the reading (pronunciation) of a character, not just the meaning. This happens naturally — by this point, you'll recognize many characters on sight and know how they sound.","zh":"<strong>阶段 4（认识约 2000 个以上的词之后）：</strong>此时，你开始根据自己能否回忆起汉字的读法（发音）来评分，而不再只看能否回忆其含义。这个转变会自然发生——到了这个阶段，你已经能一眼认出许多汉字，也知道它们怎么读。"}},{"id":"segment-0023","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"This is a gradual process. Don't rush it. The phonetic writing system is training wheels, and you take them off slowly as you build confidence.","zh":"这是一个循序渐进的过程，不要着急。表音文字系统就像自行车的辅助轮；随着信心逐渐增强，你会慢慢将它们卸下。"}},{"id":"anki-is-your-friend","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Anki Is Your Friend","zh":"Anki 是你的好帮手"}},{"id":"segment-0025","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Spaced repetition is especially important for character languages. The sheer volume of characters you need to recognize means that regular, spaced review makes a real difference. Even if you don't love Anki, finding a way to make it work for you is strongly recommended. <a href=\"/roadmap/library/learning-words-with-anki\">Learning words with Anki</a>","zh":"对于使用汉字的语言，间隔重复尤为重要。你需要辨认的汉字数量非常庞大，因此定期进行有间隔的复习确实会带来显著差异。即使你并不喜欢 Anki，也强烈建议你设法找到适合自己的使用方式。<a href=\"/roadmap/library/learning-words-with-anki\">使用 Anki 学习词语</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0026","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Some tips for making Anki work with characters: make your cards look nice (you'll be using them for years), use real native-speaker audio, and don't hesitate to delete or suspend cards that aren't working for you.","zh":"以下建议可以帮助你用 Anki 学习汉字：把卡片做得美观一些（毕竟你要使用很多年）；采用母语者的真实音频；遇到不适合自己的卡片时，不要犹豫，直接删除或暂停。"}},{"id":"dont-wait-to-start-immersing","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Don't Wait to Start Immersing","zh":"不要等准备好了才开始沉浸"}},{"id":"segment-0028","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"A common mistake is wanting to \"learn enough characters\" before starting to read in the language. Don't do this. Start immersing from day one, using materials with audio and phonetic text support. You'll be slow at first, but the exposure is what makes everything else click. Characters are learned through use, not through isolated study.","zh":"一个常见的错误是，想先“学够汉字”再开始阅读这门语言。不要这样做。从第一天起就开始沉浸，使用带有音频和注音文字辅助的材料。刚开始时你的速度会很慢，但正是这些接触会让其他知识融会贯通。汉字是在使用中学会的，而不是靠孤立学习掌握的。"}}],"cards":[]}}]
